#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "6d2095b4-1ed9-571e-8173-275bf8d56101" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3df6d520-a60b-521e-80f2-db4d3d734726" :question/math-academy-id "q-316010" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{32}{x}^{-2/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2/5+1}}{(-\\frac{2}{5}+1)}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\,{x}^{3/5}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\,{(\\sqrt[5]{x})}^{3}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({(\\sqrt[5]{32})}^{3}-{(\\sqrt[5]{1})}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({2}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d258e642-b9a6-5bf1-b482-5f152ab7aaad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d258e642-b9a6-5bf1-b482-5f152ab7aaad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232635} :answer-field/id #uuid "52f71c9c-2f6b-5370-8a49-95e60baaf004" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49847da4-c519-552f-850e-48e4275cefef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49847da4-c519-552f-850e-48e4275cefef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232637} :answer-field/id #uuid "415b1ff5-fd3e-5864-b71e-dcccb0c3c658" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232636} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3cfa260-48a2-5cf6-94ef-725c7428f7f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3cfa260-48a2-5cf6-94ef-725c7428f7f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232639} :answer-field/id #uuid "858a5c87-649c-59d5-a39d-60ba696bb7d6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16325e3c-ebce-5185-863e-0d84f168aa37" :question/math-academy-id "q-326248" :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=1-{t}^{2},\\,1\\le t\\le 6.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | $\\frac{51}{2}$ | $62$ | $\\frac{245}{2}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-3$ | {{field-3}} | $-15$ | $-24$ | $-35$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x={t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=(2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(2)=8-1=7.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=1-{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=1-(3)^{2}=1-9=-8.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(6),$ we substitute $t=6$ into $x={t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(6)=(6)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(6)=216-3=213.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n| **$t$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $7$ | $\\frac{51}{2}$ | $62$ | $\\frac{245}{2}$ | $213$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-8$ | $-15$ | $-24$ | $-35$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba3839b9-52c3-5c2d-8eb0-66915070f5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba3839b9-52c3-5c2d-8eb0-66915070f5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232653} :answer-field/id #uuid "523ba8a1-21b9-506b-9e69-898d01fca170" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232652} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "874a3212-0376-540b-8ad7-1c3642cb863a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "874a3212-0376-540b-8ad7-1c3642cb863a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232655} :answer-field/id #uuid "64e564a7-8774-510d-802b-7a665e5a8cad" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232654} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "556377da-a872-595e-bb33-f1b354b23d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "556377da-a872-595e-bb33-f1b354b23d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232657} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e4f41e-4653-5e03-81e3-e1414dff8e55" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232656} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5afb992-6a89-5dd8-a6b6-c7935703391a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5afb992-6a89-5dd8-a6b6-c7935703391a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232659} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd3b7b31-2fd8-5dc1-9e4b-cbc564bf0f26" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232658} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4485c0b2-8f41-5cc1-9591-e764086b3eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4485c0b2-8f41-5cc1-9591-e764086b3eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232661} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0451796-cc4a-5691-9da6-6d50eb33e69d" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232660} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a852a54-4426-54da-bb97-f452c07cc0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a852a54-4426-54da-bb97-f452c07cc0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232663} :answer-field/id #uuid "63e5c6e1-de4c-5909-8f20-a774fbc6e6ba" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "988a63cd-e232-53ca-b2d0-b9ce061f192c" :question/math-academy-id "q-327882" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -3,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}{{field-5}}\n$y\\le $\n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -5 & \\le 5\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 5 \\\\ -5-3 & \\le 5\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -3\\le 5-3 \\\\ -8 & \\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-8\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -7 & \\le 7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 7 \\\\ -7+1 & \\le 7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +1\\le 7+1 \\\\ -6 & \\le y\\le 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-6\\le y\\le 8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e4a72e7-508d-5d5c-9e4c-2920319fe524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e4a72e7-508d-5d5c-9e4c-2920319fe524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232913} :answer-field/id #uuid "e497afc9-ad41-544f-a9f7-7851da3ed4a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "866c7d28-7c24-5663-b483-948c59670d25" :question/math-academy-id "q-344192" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{3} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b748c5-2ab5-5931-b4cd-91a877c9d86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b748c5-2ab5-5931-b4cd-91a877c9d86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232915} :answer-field/id #uuid "56393e8a-297e-5675-99e7-f3c651936b0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85471b47-369d-5f44-8a2c-a6cf28c22b66" :question/math-academy-id "q-344238" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{-4+1}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3}}{-3}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {3}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{81}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f38b9f8c-68cb-5c08-81a8-33157e655c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f38b9f8c-68cb-5c08-81a8-33157e655c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232917} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa91474-3a5e-51df-af5a-05c71250ff69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32752764-35c0-5f79-a007-1e8e885ca82c" :question/math-academy-id "q-344240" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{1}{(-2)}) \\\\  & =-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d58ad7e4-e65a-5ca6-9241-6214d15224a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d58ad7e4-e65a-5ca6-9241-6214d15224a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232919} :answer-field/id #uuid "b77075a8-846f-512e-a9a8-403520e43188" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "be96f7f6-60a1-55ab-9eb7-614db1ea6d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-344502" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-1/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/4+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{4}+1)}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,{x}^{3/4}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(\\sqrt[4]{{4}^{3}}-\\sqrt[4]{{1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(\\sqrt[4]{64}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234186 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48cc16d0-0280-564d-bcf3-e481ad8d20ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-66126" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 1\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we write the integrand as a power of $x$, we have ${x}^{0}=1.$ Therefore, we can use the power rule with $n=0$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= 1+0 \\\\ &= 1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "280e1cee-68f8-59f3-a023-f6dd1f086b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333358} {:answer/id #uuid "c16fb7f6-a00e-595d-a28e-2f0a7397869c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334666} {:answer/id #uuid "d02e3a07-a9eb-5ee2-9205-057f65384e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334807} {:answer/id #uuid "e4417143-3102-5cc2-b8d4-db87db4085b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334978} {:answer/id #uuid "eacbc418-3efa-5b4c-a585-26704419f165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4417143-3102-5cc2-b8d4-db87db4085b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334978} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b5f293a-3b57-563b-b236-87b7649c6cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3d07c4f-77fc-5e25-9502-ae72735f0f26" :question/math-academy-id "q-6832" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right),\\,-3\\le t\\le 3,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0$ | $b$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $a$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $-a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\na=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (-3)}{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (-1)}{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (3)}{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n-a+b+c=-(-1)+(-1)+(-1)=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234256 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28dfeac9-12f2-53c4-b4b0-03e187c89f74" :question/math-academy-id "q-69923" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=-3t,\\,\\,\\,y=2t,\\,-5\\le t\\le 0,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $15$ | $12$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $a$ | $-6$ | $b$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a-b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-4$ into $y=2t.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=2(-4)=-8.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=-2$ into $y=2t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=2(-2)-4.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $x=-3t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=-3(0)=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=-8-(-4)+0=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234272 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7102fb2-1b14-588a-bd41-c065850a33f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-70412" :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$, $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $c=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $3a-(b+4c).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-(b+4c) & =3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234273 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aad6285a-b3b6-504e-bdb1-d51f41010e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-70414" :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $c=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $a+(2b-2c).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+(2b-2c) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234298 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c9da781-1702-5ac7-9948-600ab4559823" :question/math-academy-id "q-71096" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{5/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{(\\frac{5}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21fbeb51-6dc3-543d-a41d-b167ecce3c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{-6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513623} {:answer/id #uuid "3a50a15f-c07f-5fc0-8f8a-c9858dca85ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513684} {:answer/id #uuid "47d643d4-e671-5e72-9cf0-0a89cbdb8d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{1/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513726} {:answer/id #uuid "979719be-a5ae-506b-afac-4bb48471132a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{5/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513925} {:answer/id #uuid "b3abc2ef-10c8-500f-8329-7a3eb25bffce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a50a15f-c07f-5fc0-8f8a-c9858dca85ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513684} :answer-field/id #uuid "f072598f-e1c5-599b-affc-4413eaf94190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44529618-fcf7-5581-8bd3-a0df68de242f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71100" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/7}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234300 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb47b292-deba-58b8-a79e-0386e8ea55e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-71121" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=10$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{10+1}}{10+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{11}\\cdot 11{x}^{10} \\\\ &= {x}^{10}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234355 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d3103a8-b551-5ab8-b607-7a72a3223b84" :question/math-academy-id "q-73604" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-2t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=\\sqrt{t}-4,\\,t\\ge 0,\n$$\n\ndetermine the range of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-2t-1 \\\\  & ={(t-1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}-1 \\\\  & ={(t-1)}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${(t-1)}^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that $x\\ge -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182d03f6-7007-5d30-9f41-468940bf1b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t(2{t}^{2}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "182d03f6-7007-5d30-9f41-468940bf1b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t(2{t}^{2}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234577} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d80e90-19de-5aa6-bcad-6a5bb1a4fd17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f760aed-6b31-5953-b3f9-00dcac8b3038" :question/math-academy-id "q-132974" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =(2{t}^{2}+3)^{′}=4t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0647bb9-4d67-5b7d-a9c8-a4806d762874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0647bb9-4d67-5b7d-a9c8-a4806d762874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234897} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4aa3c58-8fd9-5d31-988a-048384e9e06f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cee13c2e-6226-5f1c-9ea2-cd4611314d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-167564" :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{x}$ equals\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[5]{x}={x}^{1/5}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/5+1}}{(\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5}}{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5}{x}^{6/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/5}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7acc639f-801d-5826-92a1-6c80318d504e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7acc639f-801d-5826-92a1-6c80318d504e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234734} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc6a645b-1da7-5cff-903f-cbe14bf3b0af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00bc9b45-dbe4-5490-9350-bc28715ad92a" :question/math-academy-id "q-168113" :question/problem "The divergence $\\text{div}\\,V$ of the velocity field represents the scalar rate of expansion of the fluid per unit of area at a given point. Find the corresponding expansion rate at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required rate of expansion is $\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $V=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $V$ all exist, then the divergence of $V$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot V=\\text{div}\\,V=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,V & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy) \\\\  & =2xy+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =2(1)(1)+2(1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc3ca35d-b2c9-5696-ab5b-faec203fe6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc3ca35d-b2c9-5696-ab5b-faec203fe6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234892} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a019d5-8e68-590c-aeae-b08b6bbb3998" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a254e595-25b7-5631-ae7f-2ec647939f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-168118" :question/problem "The scalar curl of a plane velocity field is called the **vorticity,** denoted $\\omega .$ The vorticity describes a measure of the local rotation of the field at a given point. Find the vorticity of $V$ at $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\omega (1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "If $V=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $V$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $V$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $P={x}^{2}y$ and $Q=2xy.$ Therefore, the scalar curl (i.e., the vorticity) of $V$ at $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega  & =\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2y-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega (1,1) & =(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =2(1)-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ca1b15-c83a-5a5b-99dd-ef8fece2b7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52ca1b15-c83a-5a5b-99dd-ef8fece2b7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234674} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eab04f3-72d0-5700-818d-000286df1e51" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cc69a41-78c7-5072-a175-2938b84fa694" :question/math-academy-id "q-168600" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n>$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor our vector field $F,$ the domain is $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=a{y}^{3}-1,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3a{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a{y}^{2} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3a & =3 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9930180-22d7-53b0-abba-b10baf047771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9930180-22d7-53b0-abba-b10baf047771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234862} :answer-field/id #uuid "744d8ba8-3ff0-5c8f-8a13-ca26a7c9ad9f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56bffa92-10bd-58ab-b621-c50b06519fae" :question/math-academy-id "q-168601" :question/problem "$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0+6xy \\\\  & =6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18784e16-207e-52ac-8f47-8998203d2c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18784e16-207e-52ac-8f47-8998203d2c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234578} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f06c954-8e61-51a6-bafe-0e589b029adb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c8722e34-4285-5973-9f7c-256551a3b9a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-168605" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6fd34e9070cd9555ba790e7300bb78b4d19f1b71b231e8a92f4e8f306c3d4623.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nThe region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. \nIn polar coordinates, the following integral can be written as\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the disk enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ec53b9b-3368-5896-a9e8-6eee1ee5ad72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ec53b9b-3368-5896-a9e8-6eee1ee5ad72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234643} :answer-field/id #uuid "a43e1a29-bf97-56d8-8130-064a3d0e5a3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "965ed3cc-e6bf-5115-8afe-a7e9b6d2886f" :question/math-academy-id "q-168615" :question/problem "$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2x+0 \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "638bdd3c-988f-5b06-9b5e-88c81e7acaff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "638bdd3c-988f-5b06-9b5e-88c81e7acaff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234699} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9aaca52-991c-54fa-a8a1-2c32329e23f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dc47c56-52c7-5494-aceb-b1baa61e89b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-168617" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required rate is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nIn part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "badc07a3-1246-57ab-8020-ef66917b8e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "badc07a3-1246-57ab-8020-ef66917b8e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234832} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66b8368-5a95-5c46-b080-d106fa6765c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1266fc86-1b5f-5a90-babf-82e935d1b03d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1266fc86-1b5f-5a90-babf-82e935d1b03d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234568} :answer-field/id #uuid "de4c731c-ed74-5645-8ab9-3c59ddda4a72" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235243} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0ef2d04-e954-5c3b-a12b-42687de0c911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0ef2d04-e954-5c3b-a12b-42687de0c911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234846} :answer-field/id #uuid "df422da9-b336-5913-a0b2-622e6c8e0a04" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5631afa-3e26-5ef4-92ef-abe2aca2eb11" :question/math-academy-id "q-168621" :question/problem "$\\text{curl}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n$⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {z}^{2} & 2xy & x{y}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xy)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(2xy-0)\\,i-({y}^{2}-2z)\\,j+(2y-0)\\,k \\\\  & =2xy\\,i-({y}^{2}-2z)\\,j+2y\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\,2z-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166d0a6c-05a8-5786-b48c-f4e93b1f3b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235955} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc3551c-3d67-5cc6-8438-b49e16bdf115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236011} {:answer/id #uuid "45c69b36-6d72-503c-9c36-09fae5919dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236267} {:answer/id #uuid "d00d1ba1-3166-54b8-b47f-c8a1f0daadff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4}(x+3)(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237136} {:answer/id #uuid "f55f988f-ed06-5427-b54b-471e782de7ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45c69b36-6d72-503c-9c36-09fae5919dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236267} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f11615a-9ff4-5a1c-97b7-4c67c3c73fb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "440dd157-2982-5c1f-8661-23a8368ce029" :question/math-academy-id "q-102664" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=1-2t,\\,\\,\\,y=(t-2)^{2}(t+1),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1-x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1-x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1-x}{2}-2)}^{2}(\\frac{1-x}{2}+1) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1-x-4}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1-x+2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-x-3}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{3-x}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77633f7b-a4f6-5605-b197-300dedbccde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(x+5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236567} {:answer/id #uuid "7b957847-885e-58d3-87c4-376c6913a3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(2x+1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236587} {:answer/id #uuid "878bf002-299b-523a-93bd-80e7642d9000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(2x+1)(2x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236663} {:answer/id #uuid "8b44f564-28f4-5786-9cf7-4b0382705e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(2x+1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236690} {:answer/id #uuid "abe07822-f677-59f4-b923-e8aa2238417d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x(x-5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77633f7b-a4f6-5605-b197-300dedbccde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(x+5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236567} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2e38393-de71-5a6b-b9b5-971eae7190b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44f36d19-593e-54ad-8307-e8844e507156" :question/math-academy-id "q-102668" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t-5}{2},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-25)(t-4),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t-5}{2}\\,⇒\\,t=2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=2x+5$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((2x+5)^{2}-25)(2x+5-4) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+20x+25-25)(2x+1) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+20x)(2x+1) \\\\  & =4x(x+5)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4x(x+5)(2x+1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c514a4-b735-5009-834b-1d072893e4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235802} {:answer/id #uuid "ac43d246-0ad4-50d7-a08c-dcc18bfc8622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236910} {:answer/id #uuid "b257feea-cffc-57ee-b376-7b4c60f365de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236946} {:answer/id #uuid "b681d134-42e1-5feb-bb0c-5e1b842860e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236973} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbed621-4c37-57a9-8a63-6518b7ef1e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b681d134-42e1-5feb-bb0c-5e1b842860e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236973} :answer-field/id #uuid "725f1b0d-a9d2-512e-a325-17a097de05d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3951ff1d-9cb6-525c-8fd5-1e1ec4a7dbb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-102680" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2663afa7-56a2-5ebd-ae11-a924299d9710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x-3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+5}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236061} {:answer/id #uuid "6395d88b-eecc-5b2d-9be3-8baa352e1b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x+2}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-3}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236449} {:answer/id #uuid "80ccf35d-bc6a-5313-875a-745c0fe7950f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x-3}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+2}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236619} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3ae7b-b354-5d77-a6b4-9ab7a0b0e1ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x-3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+2}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237341} {:answer/id #uuid "f45c9dd1-e74e-58da-9234-d134303089a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x-5}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+3}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80ccf35d-bc6a-5313-875a-745c0fe7950f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x-3}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+2}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236619} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4533903-3cfe-53ab-953a-991cc8be771f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6c004f0-b68c-5924-ab03-701737917e3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-102682" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x-3}{5} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x-3}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+2}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{x-3}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+2}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"}]}
